> I would be, but I don't know where to start, I've never contributed to this project before.

That’s actually a good first issue to dive into WeasyPrint’s code!

In this kind of issues, the first step is often to read [the specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/2020/WD-css-lists-3-20201117/#propdef-list-style-position) to understand what we have to do. Maybe there’s a detailed way to display bullets (but I’m afraid that’s a rare case where the specification doesn’t give a real algorithm).

Then, you can check what we do. The comment above gives you a good hint: the related code is in `build.py`:

https://github.com/Kozea/WeasyPrint/blob/83641f3fa647458130a2130dd42cba438a3dd603/weasyprint/formatting_structure/build.py#L357-L368

The current code applies something like `transform: translate(-100%)` to the element. It works well, except when there’s a padding or a border on the parent.

If there’s an algorithm in the specification, we have to implement it instead of our translation. If there’s no specification, we can do what we want (ie. there’s no bug in WeasyPrint, even if the rendering is different that what browsers do). But… that would be better to follow what other browsers do, at least if they all do the same. You can test different browsers in different cases to be sure that they all give the same result.

Then, you can then think of a solution that would give the same result as browsers, propose it so that I can validate it (if you’re not sure), and finally you can open a PR with this new algorithm!
@liZe thank you for the detailed response, I will look into it.
Oh gosh, I just realized my browser produces the same result and doesn't indent wrapped lines of the list items with list-style-position: outside. I'll have to investigate further.
I realized what the problem was and it had nothing to do with this bug, my bad
It'd be awesome to fully support this. Meanwhile, as a workaround, you can use a `::before` pseudo like I do (with Font Awesome's marker icons):
```css
ul {
	--icon-size: .5em;
	--gutter: .5em;
	--pseudo-gutter: .2em;
	padding: 0 0 0 var(--icon-size);
}

ul li {
	/* not fully supported WeasyPrint => use a ::before pseudo */
	padding-left: var(--gutter);
}

ul li::marker {
	font-family: "Font Awesome 6 Free", sans-serif;
	font-size: var(--icon-size);
	content: "\e0b7";
}

li::before {
	content: '';
	padding-left: var(--pseudo-gutter);
}
```
I did some try on this subject.
Today, the box containing the bullet is a children of the main `li` box. It is defined by

     if parent_style['list_style_position'] == 'outside': 
         marker_box = boxes.BlockBox.anonymous_from(box, children)
         marker_box.style['position'] = 'absolute' 
         if parent_style['direction'] == 'ltr': 
             translate_x = properties.Dimension(-100, '%') 
         else: 
             translate_x = properties.Dimension(100, '%') 
         translate_y = properties.ZERO_PIXELS 
         marker_box.style['transform'] = ( 
             ('translate', (translate_x, translate_y)),)

and so it is affected by parent padding. To fix its position we have to translate it left according to `padding-left` of `li`, as I did here:
https://github.com/luca-vercelli/WeasyPrint/commit/feae8a1ea52afd7f1adc9936ac5f69ae23b32f18

On the other side, if we move the box outside the main `li` box, we will be affected by `li` margin! We will need to translate the bullet right according to `margin-left` of `li`.

Is the second solution better than first one? Do you see other solutions?


> Is the second solution better than first one? Do you see other solutions?

Blindly following the specification is very often the right choice. But here, we have both:

>If the [list item](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-lists-3/#list-item) is a [block container](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display-4/#block-container): the marker box is a block container and is placed outside the [principal block box](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-display-4/#principal-box);

and

>Alternatively, [outside](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-lists-3/#list-style-position-outside) could lay out the marker as a previous sibling of the principal inline box.

Which probably means that it can be placed before the text line. According to their DOM, browsers put the marker before the list item content, so maybe we can keep the way it’s currently done.

> On the other side, if we move the box outside the main `li` box, we will be affected by `li` margin! We will need to translate the bullet right according to `margin-left` of `li`.

You’re right, I didn’t think of that. From the specification:

>The marker box is fixed with respect to the principal block box’s border

Either inside or outside, that won’t work cleanly with the current code.

I’m not sure about what to do. Maybe we should just do as other browsers seem to do: don’t treat markers as normal boxes, position them manually using the li’s border box position for x and baseline position for y, and ignore normal positioning properties (`display`, `position`, `padding`, `border`, `margin`, `transform`…).
(And sorry, that wasn’t a good first issue at all :/)
@liZe please take a look at my last commit. Position is recalculated from border, not from margin. It seems to work, at least for left-to-right pages.
> [@liZe](https://github.com/liZe) please take a look at my last commit. Position is recalculated from border, not from margin. It seems to work, at least for left-to-right pages.

Thanks! I opened #2425 with your commit, I added a commit on top of it to horizontally translate the marker when its layout is done.

We could add another commit to handle right-to-left correctly.

